======================================================================
SONG 1 — TRIBAL: a song about Humboldt County and the Native people of this area, the Yurok, Hupa, Karuk and Wiyot, their ceremonies and their history
======================================================================

----- A (13.7s) -----
[verse]
You think you own this dirt because you bought a deed.
But my granddad fished the Klamath before you knew how to read.
The Yurok told me where the river bends just right.
They told me how to keep the cedar baskets tight.

[chorus]
We are still here.
Yeah, we are still here.
Right where we started.

[verse]
You pushed the Wiyot off the land by the bay.
You tried to wash our talking and our dancing away.
But the Hupa kept the songs in the valley deep and low.
We waited for the wind to tell us when it was time to go.

[chorus]
We are still here.
Yeah, we are still here.
Right where we started.

[verse]
Now the Karuk are fighting for the forest and the creek.
The government is talking but they never let us speak.
We are burning the brush to make the berries grow.
We are planting the seeds that our fathers used to sow.

[chorus]
We are still here.
Yeah, we are still here.
Right where we started.

[verse]
Put your boots in the mud and look me in the eye.
You can build your fences till the river runs dry.
But the mountain remembers who walked here first.
And the land is waiting for the bubble to burst.

[chorus]
We are still here.
Yeah, we are still here.
Right where we started.

----- B (7.5s) -----
[verse]
The fog rolls in off the Pacific Coast Highway
And covers the trees with a wet gray sheet
My grandfather walked these trails when the roads were just dirt
And he told me stories about the Winter Ceremonies
He said we danced for the seals and the salmon runs
And he kept the old songs alive in his chest
Even when the government tried to break our backs
We still found ways to gather in the cold

[chorus]
We are still here
Watching the river bend
We are still here
Holding onto the end

[verse]
I went down to Humboldt Bay last Tuesday
And saw the gray whales moving slow
But I also saw the power lines cutting across the sky
And the redwood stumps rotting in the rain
My cousin sings the Basket Song at the powwow
And her hands move fast like she’s weaving the air
She wears a button blanket and she knows every name
Of the people who lived here before the settlers came

[chorus]
We are still here
Watching the river bend
We are still here
Holding onto the end

[verse]
Now the tourists take photos of the ferns and the moss
And they don’t ask who built the longhouse there
They just want a place to sell their cannabis plants
And forget that this land was stolen from us long ago
But we remember the fire sticks and the cedar bark
And we remember the names of the Chiefs who fought
We talk to the spirits of the black oak trees
And we sing until the sun comes up again

[chorus]
We are still here
Watching the river bend
We are still here
Holding onto the end

